==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|apointen}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|apointier||to prepare, arrange, lean, place}} ({{cog|fr|appointer||to give a salary, refer a cause}}), from {{der|en|la-lat|appunctō||to bring back to the point, restore, to fix the point in a controversy, or the points in an agreement}}; Latin {{m|la|ad}} + {{m|la|punctum||a point}}. See {{m|en|point}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈpɔɪnt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪnt|s=2}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-appoint.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{hyph|en|ap|point}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set, fix or [[determine]] (a time or place for something such as a [[meeting]], or the meeting itself) by [[authority]] or [[agreement]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1820|journal=The Edinburgh Annual Register
|passage=His Royal Highness called to pay his respects to her Majesty; but, from the unexpected nature of his visit, her Majesty was not in a state then to receive him; but soon after sent a letter to Prince Leopold, to '''appoint''' one o'clock this day for an interview.}}
#* {{RQ:Telegraph|date=November 8 2014|author=Ivan Hewett|title=Art on demand makes emperors of us all|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11216699/Art-on-demand-makes-emperors-of-us-all.html
|passage=We have to wait until they're ready to receive us, and make sure we turn up at the '''appointed''' time.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250926183046/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11216699/Art-on-demand-makes-emperors-of-us-all.html|archivedate=26 September 2025}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[name]] (someone to a [[post]] or [[role]]).
#: {{syn|en|make|name|designate|nominate|institute}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Numbers|4|19|passage=''Aaron and his shall go in, and '''appoint''' them every one to his service.''}}
#* {{RQ:NYPost|date=November 3 2014|author=Fredric U. Dicker|title=[http://nypost.com/2014/11/03/author-of-democrats-threatening-letter-identified/ Cuomo appointed 'vote or else' strategist]
|passage=Neal Kwatra, '''appointed''' by Cuomo to be the state Democratic Party's chief campaign strategist, was identified by two key Democratic insiders[...]}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[furnish]] or [[equip]] (a place) completely; to [[provide]] with all the [[equipment]] or [[furnishings]] necessary; to [[fit out]].
#* {{RQ:Lawrence Lady Chatterley's Lover|X|151|passage=It was a handsome old stucco hall, very elegantly '''appointed''', for Winter was a bachelor and prided himself on his style; but the place was beset by collieries.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Donald Olson|title=Germany for Dummies
|passage=The hotel is beautifully designed and beautifully '''appointed''' in a classic, modern style that manages to be both serene and luxurious at the same time.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[equip]] (someone) with (something); to [[assign]] (someone) [[authoritatively]] (some equipment).
# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To fix the [[disposition]] of ([[property]]) by [[designate|designating]] someone to take use of (it).
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To fix with [[powe]]r or [[firmness]] by [[decree]] or [[command]]; to [[ordain]] or [[establish]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Proverbs|8|29|passage=''When he gave to the sea his decree, that the waters should not pass his commandment: when he '''appointed''' the foundations of the earth:''}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Samuel|15|15|passage=''Thy servants are ready to do whatsoever my lord the king shall '''appoint'''.''}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[resolve]]; to [[determine]]; to [[ordain]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Samuel|17|14|passage=For the {{smallcaps|Lord}} had '''appointed''' to defeat the good counsel of Ahithophel.<!--compare the NIV "For the {{smallcaps|Lord}} had '''determined''' to frustrate the good advice of Ahithophel."-->}}
#*: The day being very stormy, we were obliged to keep at home; which I much regretted, as it abridged my opportunity of seeing the Jewish synagogues, as we had '''appointed''' to do to-day.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|title=The Miscellaneous Works of the Rev. Matthew Henry
|passage=He had preached twice on the Lord's day, he preached also on Monday, and had '''appointed''' to do the same on Tuesday, but died that morning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=w:Anthony Trollope|title=The Kellys and the O'Kellys|isbn=5874358048|page=251
|passage=On the following morning Lord Ballindine[,] as he had '''appointed''' to do, drove over to Dunmore, to settle with Martin about the money, and, if necessary, to go with him to the attorney's office in Tuam.}}

